# Machines that run the trials of their platform, next to this one (tools/run_all_trials.sh reads .bees_build_hosts, which is
# untracked: copy this file to .bees_build_hosts and edit it).
#
# One line per machine, whitespace separated:
#   PLATFORM_ID  USER@HOST  REMOTE_PATH  OPENSSL_PREFIX  JOBS  [NOTES]
#     PLATFORM_ID    macos_applesilicon | linux_x86_64 | ... (tools/platform_id.py)
#     USER@HOST      reached with key-based ssh in batch mode only: ssh -o BatchMode=yes USER@HOST true must work
#     REMOTE_PATH    where the BEES tree is synced to (absolute, or starting with ~); a Silica checkout must sit beside it
#                    (SILICA_ROOT, default ../silica of the tree) or NOTES must say where
#     OPENSSL_PREFIX the programmer-installed OpenSSL 4.1.0 or later there (passed as OPENSSL_PREFIX); - for none
#     JOBS           trials at a time on that machine; - for this machine's -j
#     NOTES          free text; the NAME=VALUE tokens SILICA_COMPILER, SILICA_ROOT, BEES_COMPILE_JOBS, SILICA_COMPILE_TIMEOUT
#                    are passed to the remote run's environment
#
# NEVER put a key, a password or a token in this file (the reader refuses a line that mentions one).
#
# SILICA_COMPILER must name a compiler of that machine's platform (the default binaries/silica-compiler of a checkout copied from a
# Mac is the macOS one). The box below has 2 cores and about 3.9 GB: 2 trials at a time (a compile peaks near 300 MB).
linux_x86_64 manager@aalang.org /opt/bees /opt/openssl 2 SILICA_COMPILER=/opt/silica/binaries/silica-compiler-linux_x86_64 SILICA_ROOT=/opt/silica Linux x86_64, the first remote machine
